        <h6>
            <a class="me-1" href="/search/en/records/6a96d3e739c6c869cfbcb18b" data-agris-title>
                Epigenetic bias: Maternal CpG methylation signatures linked to sex-biased progeny and paternal mitochondrial fate in Mytilus galloprovincialis

                
                
            </a>
            
            <span class="badge bg-emergency">Full text</span>

            
        </h6>

        
        <p class="m-0 p-0">2027</p>

        
        <p>García-Souto, Daniel | Diz, Ángel P. | Morán, Paloma | Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades (España) | Xunta de Galicia | European Commission | Universidad de Vigo</p>

        
        
        <div class="abstract mb-3">
            <p id="abstract_6a96d3e739c6c869cfbcb18b" class="m-0" aria-expanded="false"
               data-agris-abstract>
                12 pages, 7 tables, 4 figures.-- Raw sequencing data, including POD5 files, have been deposited in the NCBI Sequence Read Archive (SRA) under the bioproject ID PRJNA1453758 | DNA methylation is a conserved epigenetic modification involved in gene regulation and development, yet its role in sex determination and mitochondrial inheritance in bivalves remains poorly understood. In mussels of the genus Mytilus, sex determination is associated with Doubly Uniparental Inheritance (DUI) of mitochondria, in which paternal mitochondria are selectively retained in males. Here, we examined genome-wide CpG methylation in mature female gonads of Mytilus galloprovincialis producing strongly sex-biased progeny. Global nuclear methylation was low (∼11%) and concentrated in gene bodies and repetitive elements, whereas mitochondrial CpG methylation was nearly absent. A preliminary comparison of son- and daughter-biased females identified 28 genes and 15 Penelope-like retroelements showing differential methylation, most hypomethylated in son-biased females. These loci were enriched in functions related to retrovirus-derived elements, and DNA repair and processing. Overall, our results suggest that maternal DNA methylation may be associated with progeny sex bias and mitochondrial inheritance in marine mussels, providing the first genome-wide methylome characterization of mature female gonads in M. galloprovincialis | This work was supported by grants PID2019-107611RB-I00 and PID2024-155396OB-I00 from MICIU/AEI/10.13039/501100011033, and by Xunta de Galicia (GRC-ED431C 2024/22, and ED431G 2023/07 “Centro singular de investigación de Galicia”), including funding from FEDER (ERDF, European Union). Funding for open access charge: Universidade de Vigo/CISUG | Peer reviewed
            </p>
            <a role="button" class="collapsed float-right" style="font-size: .9rem;"
