        <h6>
            <a class="me-1" href="/search/en/records/6a96d3e739c6c869cfbcb18c" data-agris-title>
                Genetic architecture and genomic regions associated with malpigmentation in farmed turbot (Scophthalmus maximus)

                
                
            </a>
            
            <span class="badge bg-emergency">Full text</span>

            
        </h6>

        
        <p class="m-0 p-0">2027</p>

        
        <p>Costas Imbernón, Daniel | Saura, María | Otero, Susana | Guerrero-Peña, Laura | Megías, Manuel | Touriñán, Pablo | García-Fernández, Pablo | Tur, R. | Chavarrías, David | Rotllant, Josep | Ministerio de Ciencia e Innovación (España) | European Commission</p>

        
        
        <div class="abstract mb-3">
            <p id="abstract_6a96d3e739c6c869cfbcb18c" class="m-0" aria-expanded="false"
               data-agris-abstract>
                13 pages, 8 figures, 4 tables.-- Data will be made available on request | Pigmentation disorders are a major constraint in turbot aquaculture, reducing the commercial value of affected fish. In turbot, defects mainly manifest as pseudoalbinism on the ocular side and hypermelanosis on the blind side. Here, we present the first comprehensive study dissecting the genetic architecture of malpigmentation in this species. We analyzed 785 juveniles from 10 full- and half-sib families, phenotyped using both a binary score and a continuous image-based index, genotyped with a 5K SNP chip, and imputed to 1.68 million SNPs using whole-genome resequencing of 19 parents. Genomic heritability was moderate to high (h2 ≈ 0.4–0.7), suggesting a strong additive genetic component. While the SNP-chip identified only two significant signals, the imputed dataset substantially increased mapping resolution, revealing 319 and 295 significant SNPs for the binary and continuous traits, respectively, including extended linkage disequilibrium blocks on chromosomes 5 and 15. Functional annotation uncovered canonical pigmentation loci and pathways involved in melanocyte differentiation, neural crest development, calcium and MAPK signalling, and endocrine regulation. Together, these findings establish the first genomic framework for malpigmentation in turbot, providing actionable markers and a robust foundation for genomic selection strategies to improve product quality, uniformity, and economic sustainability in aquaculture | This work was supported by MCIN/AEI/10.13039/501100011033 (PID2021-1236511OB-100) with funding by Next Generation EU/PRTR | Peer reviewed
            </p>
            <a role="button" class="collapsed float-right" style="font-size: .9rem;"
