        <h6>
            <a class="me-1" href="/search/en/records/6aac06fe6ec95fdd024713a4" data-agris-title>
                Bile salts govern protein bioaccessibility and nanostructural assembly during digestion of Nori seaweed

                
                
            </a>
            
            <span class="badge bg-emergency">Full text</span>

            
        </h6>

        
        <p class="m-0 p-0">2027</p>

        
        <p>Correa, Yubexi | Cebrián-Lloret, Vera | López-Sánchez, Patricia | Martínez, Juan Carlos | López-Rubio, Amparo | Fevola, Giovanni | Alba Venero, Diego | Zhang, Yi | Greving, Imke | Flenner, Silja | Martínez Sanz, Marta | European Commission | European Research Council | UK Research and Innovation | State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation | Agencia Estatal de Investigación | Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades</p>

        
        
        <div class="abstract mb-3">
            <p id="abstract_6aac06fe6ec95fdd024713a4" class="m-0" aria-expanded="false"
               data-agris-abstract>
                Seaweeds are increasingly recognized as sustainable and nutritious candidates to support future food systems, yet the mechanisms governing their digestibility remain poorly understood. In this work, we show that the cell walls of Nori seaweed (Porphyra umbilicalis) are highly resistant to gastric digestion, with protein release occurring primarily during the intestinal phase. Using a multiscale analytical approach, including advanced tools such as small-angle X-ray and neutron scattering and nanotomography, we tracked in vitro digestion-induced nanoscale structural reorganization. We found that porphyran-rich extracellular networks restrict enzyme accessibility during gastric digestion, whereas bile salts in the intestinal environment promote polysaccharide and protein solubilization, enhancing proteolysis. These interactions, in turn, hinder the structural ordering and correlation of bile salt nanomicelles in the digesta. These findings reveal the key role of bile salts in the digestion process, not only governing protein digestibility, but also influencing the nanostructuring of digestion products. More broadly, this work establishes a mechanistic framework to investigate how nanoscale self-assembly during digestion influences nutrient transport and physiological responses. | This work was funded by the European Union (ERC, PRODIGEST, ERC-2022-COG 101086483). Views and opinions expressed are, however, those of the author(s) only and do not necessarily reflect those of the European Union or the European Research Council Executive Agency. Neither the European Union nor the granting authority can be held responsible for them. Synchrotron experiments were performed at the NCD beamline at ALBA Synchrotron with the collaboration of ALBA staff (proposals 2023077631 and 2024078506). SANS experiments were performed at ISIS Neutron and Muon Source (RB2428001; data available: https://doi.org/10.5286/ISIS.E.RB2428001), cryo-TEM characterization of digesta was done at the Life Science EM facility of the Ernst-Ruska Centre at Forschungszentrum Jülich. We acknowledge Helmholtz-Zentrum Hereon and DESY, members of the Helmholtz Association HGF, for the provision of the experimental infrastructure and the provision of beamtime at P05 at PETRA III for proposal(s) PID30299. This research was supported in part through the Maxwell computational resources operated at DESY. All of these experiments were funded by the REMADE proposal PID30299, which was funded by the European Union as part of the Horizon Europe call HORIZON-INFRA-2021-SERV-01 under grant agreement number 101058414 and co-funded by UK Research and Innovation (UKRI) under the UK government&#39;s Horizon Europe funding guarantee (grant number 10039728) and by the Swiss State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation (SERI) under contract number 22.00187. Views and opinions expressed are, however, those of the author(s) only and do not necessarily reflect those of the European Union, the UK Science and Technology Facilities Council or the Swiss State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation (SERI). Neither the European Union nor the granting authorities can be held responsible for them. Vera Cebrián was supported by a postdoctoral fellowship funded by the Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades from Spain (JDC2024-053925-I). The Accreditation of IATA-CSIC as Center of Excellence Severo Ochoa CEX2021- 001189-S funded by MCIN/AEI/10.13039/501100011033 is also fully acknowledged. | With funding from the Spanish government through the &#34;Severo Ochoa Centre of Excellence&#34; accreditation (CEX2021-001189-S). | Peer reviewed
            </p>
